In older languages, repeating an action three times meant babysitting a manual counter: start `i` at `0`, test whether `i` is below `3`, bump `i` by `1`, and hope you didn't miss by one. Matz asked a charming question: if `3` is already a full object, why not hand `3` a chunk of code and tell it to run that code three times? That insight gave birth to Ruby's beloved **first-class block**—a portable snippet of executable code wrapped in `do ... end` or `{ ... }` that you can pass directly into any method call while automatically remembering local variables from its surroundings:

```ruby
3.times { puts "Hello!" }
long_titles = books.select { |b| b.length > 5 }
```

Here `puts` prints a line and `|b|` names each book handed to the block. Rather than pulling data into awkward manual loops, you send *behavior* straight to the object—and `File.open("notes.txt") { |f| ... }` automatically closes the file the moment your block finishes!
